    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "25da3608_4398fbcf",
        "filename": "src/shaders/SkShader.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-21T18:59:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If `workingSpace` is null, should it instead be assumed to be sRGB? That matches our conventions on other CS inputs. It\u0027s probably frequently the same as being the dst color space but could be useful to ensure you\u0027re in sRGB going to a wider gamut.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "e444f72ae7bd21d4306d760f4fa30587e5b9f249",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "64aeb232_d4f79a99",
        "filename": "src/shaders/SkShader.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-21T19:27:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I debated about this one (and I could probably go either way). Our actual pattern for color-spaces is that a null src means sRGB. A null dst means \"same as source\" (in other words, no color management). The working color space here is somewhat ambiguous. You could interpret is as \"this is the new source color space for this shader\", so nullptr -\u003e sRGB would make sense. You could also interpret it to mean \"pretend that we drew this thing to a new destination with this color space before drawing it to the real destination\", which is closer to how it really operates, and in which case leaving nullptr as-is is closer than using sRGB.\n\nOne of the big uses for this feature *is* to run certain filters in definite sRGB, but I also assume any customer that knows they want that is going to be explicit about it. Overall, I\u0027m tempted to leave it like this.",
      "parentUuid": "25da3608_4398fbcf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "e444f72ae7bd21d4306d760f4fa30587e5b9f249",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c11b81b_10e039bc",
        "filename": "src/shaders/SkShader.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-21T19:55:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah that\u0027s fair. If you want to be definite about operating in sRGB, it makes the most sense to just pass in SkColorSpace::MakeSRGB()",
